WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.330
This briefing comes from Book 15 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

2
00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:07.112
Compliance Automation
and Day-Two Operations.

3
00:00:07.112 --> 00:00:10.842
It's written for C I Os, service
desk and operations leads,

4
00:00:10.842 --> 00:00:13.762
and information system security officers.

5
00:00:13.762 --> 00:00:19.095
I'll take four questions: why modernization
succeeds or fails at the help desk;

6
00:00:19.095 --> 00:00:23.512
what day two runs on; who runs it;
and how to run it and prove it works.

7
00:00:23.512 --> 00:00:27.224
The short version: cutover proves
the new estate can be built.

8
00:00:27.224 --> 00:00:31.600
The first locked-out user on Monday
morning proves whether it can be run.

9
00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:36.656
A modernization program spends
most of its attention on day one:

10
00:00:36.656 --> 00:00:40.800
the tenant is built, identities
synchronize, the cutover runs.

11
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.359
The estate is judged on
day two, when every reset,

12
00:00:44.359 --> 00:00:47.462
unlock and new hire
lands at the help desk.

13
00:00:47.462 --> 00:00:50.837
In Entra ID the help
desk's power is explicit.

14
00:00:50.837 --> 00:00:55.594
Microsoft labels the Helpdesk Administrator
role privileged, and warns that

15
00:00:55.594 --> 00:01:00.276
changing a user's password may mean the
ability to assume that user's identity.

16
00:01:00.276 --> 00:01:03.943
Requiring M F A re-registration
deletes the user's phones,

17
00:01:03.943 --> 00:01:07.299
Authenticator registrations
and software tokens,

18
00:01:07.299 --> 00:01:11.825
and whoever signs in next and registers
a method controls the account.

19
00:01:11.825 --> 00:01:16.050
So a reset on an unverified
request isn't a support task.

20
00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.862
It's a transfer of the
account to whoever asked.

21
00:01:18.862 --> 00:01:20.062
Attackers know it.

22
00:01:20.062 --> 00:01:24.673
CISA's advisory on the Scattered
Spider group describes calls that

23
00:01:24.673 --> 00:01:29.363
convinced help desk staff to reset
passwords and transfer M F A tokens.

24
00:01:29.363 --> 00:01:33.322
And when the governed path is
hard, the desk works around it:

25
00:01:33.322 --> 00:01:37.199
an emergency admin account, an
email treated as a request,

26
00:01:37.199 --> 00:01:40.860
a temporary password sent
back to the same inbox.

27
00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:45.185
Two federal documents frame
the reset and recovery path.

28
00:01:45.185 --> 00:01:50.721
OMB's zero trust memorandum, M-22-09,
requires phishing-resistant M F A,

29
00:01:50.721 --> 00:01:55.430
and then names the hole: recovery
processes are a potential bypass of

30
00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:00.635
standard authentication, and a significant
threat vector if not mitigated.

31
00:02:00.635 --> 00:02:04.783
It asks for high-friction methods
that are costly for an adversary,

32
00:02:04.783 --> 00:02:07.573
such as in-person
verification or live video.

33
00:02:07.573 --> 00:02:10.395
NIST's digital identity guidelines,

34
00:02:10.395 --> 00:02:14.347
revised in July 2025, give
recovery its own section.

35
00:02:14.347 --> 00:02:17.740
They recognize four methods:
saved recovery codes,

36
00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:22.748
issued recovery codes, recovery
contacts, and repeated identity proofing.

37
00:02:22.748 --> 00:02:27.378
A help desk agent is allowed only on
the basis of a documented risk analysis,

38
00:02:27.378 --> 00:02:30.962
repeated proofing has to be
consistent with the original,

39
00:02:30.962 --> 00:02:34.098
and every recovery must
notify the account holder.

40
00:02:34.098 --> 00:02:38.697
Read together, the help desk reset has
to be an exception, not the main path;

41
00:02:38.697 --> 00:02:40.996
high-friction for the account's risk;

42
00:02:40.996 --> 00:02:45.748
at least as strong as the original
proofing; and always followed by a notice.

43
00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:49.880
Email, caller I D and knowledge
questions verify no one.

44
00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:54.280
The same argument holds from the
side of the authorization package.

45
00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:57.805
An authorization describes
the system as it was assessed.

46
00:02:57.805 --> 00:03:01.905
The day after, the help desk
resets passwords, onboards people,

47
00:03:01.905 --> 00:03:07.532
changes groups and fixes a synchronization
error, and each change touches a control:

48
00:03:07.532 --> 00:03:11.150
account management, least
privilege, authenticators,

49
00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:14.205
change control, the
inventory, the audit trail.

50
00:03:14.205 --> 00:03:18.646
Done as portal clicks, each leaves
no record the control recognizes,

51
00:03:18.646 --> 00:03:21.105
and the package ages with every click.

52
00:03:21.105 --> 00:03:25.330
The moderate baseline asks for
automation only in specific places,

53
00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:29.705
so the book's emphasis on automation
is a design position about volume.

54
00:03:29.705 --> 00:03:33.966
At day-two volume, people assembling
the record by hand can't keep up,

55
00:03:33.966 --> 00:03:37.530
and what they produce is a
reconstruction, not evidence.

56
00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:41.921
A control passes three layers:
designed, deployed as code,

57
00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:46.592
and operated, with an owner, a
gate, a clock and an evidence feed.

58
00:03:46.592 --> 00:03:52.086
Every operation starts from the source of
truth, carries the approval its control

59
00:03:52.086 --> 00:03:57.418
requires, runs under a just-in-time role,
closes with proof and emits its evidence.

60
00:03:57.418 --> 00:04:00.410
Miss one, and the work can't be attested.

61
00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:04.260
Now what day two runs on,
starting at the front door.

62
00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.297
Nothing is handled as ad hoc
email to the cloud services team.

63
00:04:08.297 --> 00:04:13.040
Self-service requests, the team mailbox,
the identity governance platform and

64
00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.073
monitoring alerts all land in one triage
queue in the service management platform.

65
00:04:18.073 --> 00:04:21.563
The mailbox rule has to
redirect, not blind-copy,

66
00:04:21.563 --> 00:04:25.322
or there are two queues;
pilot it in audit mode first.

67
00:04:25.322 --> 00:04:29.572
A reroute rule pulls back anything
assigned straight to cloud services.

68
00:04:29.572 --> 00:04:33.660
Triage asks one question: what
authority does this task need?

69
00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.135
No access change is class A0.

70
00:04:36.135 --> 00:04:40.710
A change to the requester's own account
is A1, and the manager approves.

71
00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.910
Access an owner governs is A2.

72
00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:48.010
Governance actions, such as admin
consent, are A3 and never automated.

73
00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:51.198
Conditional Access and
directory roles escalate.

74
00:04:51.198 --> 00:04:55.552
The checks run in a fixed order, so
the dangerous cases are caught first:

75
00:04:55.552 --> 00:05:00.734
anything claiming to come from Microsoft,
then any target holding a directory role.

76
00:05:00.734 --> 00:05:05.510
And an approval nobody answers
expires to reject, never to approve.

77
00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:09.651
In Entra ID, three things decide
what a technician can do:

78
00:05:09.651 --> 00:05:13.705
the role decides the action, the
target decides whether it applies,

79
00:05:13.705 --> 00:05:16.948
and the administrative unit
narrows the population.

80
00:05:16.948 --> 00:05:22.060
The least-privileged role for a non-admin
password reset is Password Administrator.

81
00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:25.087
Authentication Administrator
handles methods

82
00:05:25.087 --> 00:05:27.597
and Temporary Access Passes for members.

83
00:05:27.597 --> 00:05:31.221
Only the Privileged Authentication
Administrator can reset a

84
00:05:31.221 --> 00:05:34.709
privileged administrator, so
that role sits outside the desk.

85
00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:36.685
Capability isn't authority.

86
00:05:36.685 --> 00:05:40.259
Authentication Administrator
can delete and disable users,

87
00:05:40.259 --> 00:05:44.043
and Application Administrator
can grant consent, so the program

88
00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:48.247
replaces it with a custom role that
only assigns groups to applications.

89
00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:53.173
Executives sit in restricted management
units the general desk can't reach.

90
00:05:53.173 --> 00:05:58.284
Roles reach technicians through
role-assignable groups, eligible through P I M.

91
00:05:58.284 --> 00:06:00.910
And approval isn't identity verification.

92
00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:05.600
The ladder is the CISO's decision:
a phishing-resistant sign-in first;

93
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.973
identity proofing, in person
against a PIV card or

94
00:06:08.973 --> 00:06:12.922
through Entra account recovery,
when every method is lost.

95
00:06:12.922 --> 00:06:17.858
Then a one-time pass with the shortest
lifetime, a notice on a separate channel,

96
00:06:17.858 --> 00:06:21.990
and for high-risk tasks, two
approvers besides the executor.

97
00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:25.778
Every routine change
arrives as a typed request.

98
00:06:25.778 --> 00:06:31.078
The help desk catalog has ten items, for
joiners, movers, leavers, credentials,

99
00:06:31.078 --> 00:06:35.285
groups, licenses, Conditional
Access exceptions and guests,

100
00:06:35.285 --> 00:06:40.502
each bound to the control it exercises
and the approval that control needs.

101
00:06:40.502 --> 00:06:44.690
Each item closes only when a read
of the target confirms the change.

102
00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:49.310
The leaver fails audits most often:
disable the account, revoke sessions,

103
00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:54.315
deprovision every connected application,
and reassign what the person owned.

104
00:06:54.315 --> 00:06:58.286
And federal 911 rules bind
whoever operates the phone system,

105
00:06:58.286 --> 00:07:02.490
so a validated emergency address
gates every number assignment.

106
00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:05.090
SaaS integration is the densest moment.

107
00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:09.863
In GCC, an administrator sees the full
commercial application gallery and can

108
00:07:09.863 --> 00:07:13.315
federate a third-party service
in about fifteen minutes,

109
00:07:13.315 --> 00:07:16.840
and nothing in the vendor's
path asks about authorization.

110
00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:19.559
Restricting consent
decides who may approve,

111
00:07:19.559 --> 00:07:22.278
not whether the service
may hold federal data.

112
00:07:22.278 --> 00:07:27.802
So the catalog records a FedRAMP Marketplace
verdict before anything is configured,

113
00:07:27.802 --> 00:07:32.280
with Class C or higher as the
program's default for identity data.

114
00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:34.929
Every ticket names an asset,

115
00:07:34.929 --> 00:07:39.305
so the help desk can only be as
right as the inventory it routes to.

116
00:07:39.305 --> 00:07:42.965
The configuration database
earns that trust by agreeing

117
00:07:42.965 --> 00:07:46.092
with one authoritative
identity, not by being one.

118
00:07:46.092 --> 00:07:48.514
Discovery and connectors populate it;

119
00:07:48.514 --> 00:07:51.768
the naming and addressing
record governs identity.

120
00:07:51.768 --> 00:07:56.930
Where the two disagree, the database is
wrong, and the disagreement is a finding.

121
00:07:56.930 --> 00:08:01.092
The inventory control asks for no
duplicate accounting of components.

122
00:08:01.092 --> 00:08:05.619
ServiceNow's identification and
reconciliation engine already prevents

123
00:08:05.619 --> 00:08:09.643
duplicates and lets only authorized
sources write each attribute.

124
00:08:09.643 --> 00:08:15.063
The program adds one pass: match each
asset to the naming record first, bind it,

125
00:08:15.063 --> 00:08:19.021
and flag the rest, either a
shadow asset nobody approved,

126
00:08:19.021 --> 00:08:23.581
or a gap that's fixed in address
management, never in the database.

127
00:08:23.581 --> 00:08:26.129
Duplicates are resolved by binding,

128
00:08:26.129 --> 00:08:29.442
not deleting, so no open
finding loses its asset.

129
00:08:29.442 --> 00:08:33.092
Stale items are disabled and
attested before they're retired.

130
00:08:33.092 --> 00:08:35.456
It matters because a fix that lands on one

131
00:08:35.456 --> 00:08:38.167
duplicate leaves the
finding open on the other.

132
00:08:38.167 --> 00:08:42.740
And watch the connector that quietly
returns half its usual records.

133
00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:47.084
Microsoft, AWS and VMware
already ship tools that

134
00:08:47.084 --> 00:08:50.590
test most of the settings
an assessor asks about.

135
00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:54.766
The gap is turning each failed
test into owned work with a clock,

136
00:08:54.766 --> 00:08:57.015
and each closed task into a record.

137
00:08:57.015 --> 00:09:02.259
The program runs one loop everywhere:
the native tool tests, drift is detected,

138
00:09:02.259 --> 00:09:05.947
a typed task is raised against
the configuration item,

139
00:09:05.947 --> 00:09:11.027
the owning team fixes it natively, and
the task closes on a re-test, not a click.

140
00:09:11.027 --> 00:09:15.264
The service management platform reads
control state; it never writes it.

141
00:09:15.264 --> 00:09:16.953
The government clouds differ.

142
00:09:16.953 --> 00:09:21.828
Defender for Cloud's ServiceNow integration
isn't available in Azure Government.

143
00:09:21.828 --> 00:09:27.473
Compliance Manager's continuous
assessments are still rolling out in GCC.

144
00:09:27.473 --> 00:09:31.479
AWS Audit Manager closed to
new customers on April 30,

145
00:09:31.479 --> 00:09:34.028
2026, and has no GovCloud endpoint.

146
00:09:34.028 --> 00:09:38.340
AWS's ServiceNow connector
loses support on March 31,

147
00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:42.752
2027, and Security Hub keeps
findings only ninety days.

148
00:09:42.752 --> 00:09:47.133
In VMware Cloud Foundation 9.1,
the compliance benchmarks were

149
00:09:47.133 --> 00:09:51.514
replaced by a posture tool that
lists no NIST or DISA benchmark.

150
00:09:51.514 --> 00:09:56.600
On premises, the agency produces
every piece of evidence itself.

151
00:09:57.350 --> 00:10:02.075
Before anyone builds a compliance application,
know what the platform already does.

152
00:10:02.075 --> 00:10:05.057
ServiceNow's Policy and
Compliance Management

153
00:10:05.057 --> 00:10:08.263
schedules control tests
and turns gaps into issues.

154
00:10:08.263 --> 00:10:13.661
Continuous Authorization and Monitoring
walks the seven steps of the Risk Management

155
00:10:13.661 --> 00:10:18.542
Framework, gives one view of every plan
of action, and in recent releases imports

156
00:10:18.542 --> 00:10:23.201
and exports security plans, plans of
action and assessment results in OSCAL.

157
00:10:23.201 --> 00:10:26.557
Configuration Compliance
imports Defender for Cloud

158
00:10:26.557 --> 00:10:29.550
results and matches them
to configuration items.

159
00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:33.975
The government offering is listed as
FedRAMP Certified at Class D, High.

160
00:10:33.975 --> 00:10:38.969
So the recommendation is to buy the native
applications first and build only what

161
00:10:38.969 --> 00:10:43.156
they don't do: join every finding to
the authoritative asset identity,

162
00:10:43.156 --> 00:10:46.241
close only with proof and
flag human-only closures,

163
00:10:46.241 --> 00:10:49.913
and check the platform's control
set against the control map.

164
00:10:49.913 --> 00:10:54.500
A control is proven by system evidence
wherever a system can see it;

165
00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:57.837
people attest only to what
no system can observe.

166
00:10:57.837 --> 00:11:00.620
And FedRAMP's indicators bind providers;

167
00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:04.490
for the agency they're
vocabulary, not a deliverable.

168
00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:09.979
Day two is run by the people who touch the
estate most often and decide the least,

169
00:11:09.979 --> 00:11:13.989
so every decision sits with someone
who isn't holding the keyboard,

170
00:11:13.989 --> 00:11:16.978
and every artifact has one
owner and a review date.

171
00:11:16.978 --> 00:11:19.115
The service desk has three lines.

172
00:11:19.115 --> 00:11:22.100
The first takes every
request, classifies it,

173
00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:26.215
verifies the person and executes
against an approved ticket.

174
00:11:26.215 --> 00:11:30.027
Cloud services is the escalation
tier, not a second queue.

175
00:11:30.027 --> 00:11:31.627
Technicians approve nothing.

176
00:11:31.627 --> 00:11:36.103
The supervisor approves routine
credential work for ordinary users,

177
00:11:36.103 --> 00:11:37.878
never a ticket they execute.

178
00:11:37.878 --> 00:11:41.903
Platform owners own the runbooks
and what each catalog item does.

179
00:11:41.903 --> 00:11:45.923
The control owner, the CISO or
the identity governance officer,

180
00:11:45.923 --> 00:11:47.365
owns the approval gate.

181
00:11:47.365 --> 00:11:51.015
The service management team
builds both and decides neither:

182
00:11:51.015 --> 00:11:55.953
changing an approval rule is a normal change
the change advisory board authorizes.

183
00:11:55.953 --> 00:11:59.940
The configuration database owner
keeps the model, not the facts.

184
00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:04.308
The board has security and privacy
members and authorizes changes,

185
00:12:04.308 --> 00:12:09.065
but doesn't accept risk; that goes to
the CISO or the authorizing official.

186
00:12:09.065 --> 00:12:12.378
A contracted desk fits,
because contractors prepare

187
00:12:12.378 --> 00:12:15.740
and execute while government
officials decide.

188
00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:21.590
The runbook turns all of this into a
cadence, and each step says what it proves.

189
00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:26.998
Daily, a read-only synchronization probe
replaces the morning interactive logon to

190
00:12:26.998 --> 00:12:31.325
the sync servers, and each error
becomes a task; a completeness check

191
00:12:31.325 --> 00:12:36.115
confirms every log source is still
sending; catalog tasks close with proof.

192
00:12:36.115 --> 00:12:40.523
Weekly, detection rules are tested
against replayed known-bad events,

193
00:12:40.523 --> 00:12:45.540
and every task closed without automated
proof is either evidenced or reopened.

194
00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:49.666
Monthly, results and the plan
of action are regenerated,

195
00:12:49.666 --> 00:12:52.715
and the I S S O reviews
the harness results.

196
00:12:52.715 --> 00:12:56.140
Quarterly, privileged access
and trends are reviewed.

197
00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:59.702
The runbook also rewrites
eight everyday tasks.

198
00:12:59.702 --> 00:13:03.641
A password reset is delivered on a
channel separate from the request.

199
00:13:03.641 --> 00:13:09.078
A requester's script is never pasted and
run: parameters travel, commands don't.

200
00:13:09.078 --> 00:13:11.640
The harnesses check the automation itself.

201
00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:15.502
A source past its freshness limit
raises a high-severity alert.

202
00:13:15.502 --> 00:13:19.302
A deployment that doesn't close
its control fails the pipeline.

203
00:13:19.302 --> 00:13:22.776
And a harness that stops running
looks like one that passes,

204
00:13:22.776 --> 00:13:24.640
so check that each one ran.

205
00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:26.765
So, four decisions.

206
00:13:26.765 --> 00:13:30.354
Put the identity-verification
procedure under the CISO,

207
00:13:30.354 --> 00:13:34.092
and treat every authenticator
reset as an account transfer:

208
00:13:34.092 --> 00:13:38.802
the most friction on the most sensitive
accounts, and an alert on every reset.

209
00:13:38.802 --> 00:13:40.190
Build one front door.

210
00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:43.359
Redirect the team mailbox,
triage by authority,

211
00:13:43.359 --> 00:13:46.528
and let unanswered
approvals expire to reject.

212
00:13:46.528 --> 00:13:49.928
Buy the platform's native
compliance applications first,

213
00:13:49.928 --> 00:13:51.628
and build only what they lack.

214
00:13:51.628 --> 00:13:56.281
Record an authorization verdict before
any SaaS integration is configured,

215
00:13:56.281 --> 00:13:58.753
and check every feature in your own cloud.

216
00:13:58.753 --> 00:14:01.593
And fund the third
layer: an owner, a gate,

217
00:14:01.593 --> 00:14:04.966
a clock and an evidence
feed for every control.

218
00:14:04.966 --> 00:14:07.165
The next step needs no procurement.

219
00:14:07.165 --> 00:14:12.081
Measure before you build: run the
read-only integration inventory and

220
00:14:12.081 --> 00:14:17.421
synchronization probe in a test tenant,
size the catalog from what they find,

221
00:14:17.421 --> 00:14:22.253
and plan the move off AWS's ServiceNow
connector before March 31, 2027.

222
00:14:22.253 --> 00:14:26.953
Operations is where modernization
becomes routine or quietly reverts.

223
00:14:26.953 --> 00:14:32.564
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
15 has the detail, every claim footnoted.

224
00:14:32.564 --> 00:14:33.380
Thank you.
